    {"id": 1, "claim": "Soil formation (pedogenesis) is the process of creating new soil material from parent material, which includes both the weathering of rocks and the decomposition of organic matter into humus.", "evidence": "Standard definition of soil science: soil consists of mineral particles and organic matter.", "weight": 0.15, "depends_on": []},
    {"id": 2, "claim": "Biological decomposition of organic matter (like a rotting log) is a significantly faster process than the physical or chemical weathering of solid rock.", "evidence": "A log can decompose substantially in months to years, whereas significant weathering of a solid rock face takes decades to centuries.", "weight": 0.35, "depends_on": [1]},
    {"id": 3, "claim": "Water running in a stream primarily acts as a transport mechanism for existing sediments rather than a generator of new soil material in situ.", "evidence": "Fluvial processes move eroded particles; they do not create new mineral or organic matter at the site of flow, unlike decomposition or active weathering.", "weight": 0.25, "depends_on": [1]},
    {"id": 4, "claim": "A rock sitting in a garden undergoes extremely slow weathering due to the lack of dynamic mechanical forces and limited biological activity compared to decomposing organic matter.", "evidence": "Static rock weathering rates are orders of magnitude slower than biological decomposition rates.", "weight": 0.1, "depends_on": [1, 2]},
    {"id": 5, "claim": "Waves breaking on a beach cause erosion and redistribution of existing sand and rock fragments, but do not generate new soil material.", "evidence": "Coastal wave action is a mechanical transport/erosion process, not a pedogenic (soil-forming) process that creates new matter.", "weight": 0.1, "depends_on": [1, 3]},
    {"id": 6, "claim": "Therefore, the rotting log forms new soil (specifically the organic/humus component) the fastest because biological decomposition is the most rapid material transformation process among the options.", "evidence": "Comparison of rates: Biological decomposition (years) >> Mechanical/Chemical Weathering (decades-centuries) > Transport (no new material creation).", "weight": 0.05, "depends_on": [2, 3, 4, 5]}
  ],
  "answer": "A",
